  "limitations": [
    "Laboratory experiment results were inconsistent, possibly due to artificial setting and short exposure time.",
    "Illuminance levels (455-572 lux) in the field experiment were within the range of detectable difference, but the combined effect of illuminance and CCT needs further verification.",
    "Exposure time for each lighting condition (2-3 minutes) was much shorter than normal class periods, requiring verification for longer exposure durations and ideal exposure time.",
    "Performance decreases when arousal becomes too high (Yerkes-Dodson Law), requiring verification for longer exposure periods.",
    "Supplementary studies with different age groups are necessary for general learning environments.",
    "Total visual appearance, including wall colors, should be investigated for practical application."
  ],
  "minimum_safe_summary": "This study investigated the effects of lighting color temperatures (CCTs) on elementary students' performance through three empirical studies: a preliminary physiological study, a laboratory experiment, and a field experiment. The preliminary study found that 6500 K CCT increased physiological arousal (LF/HF ratio) while 3500 K CCT was most relaxing. The laboratory experiment yielded inconsistent results for cognitive and behavioral performance, possibly due to artificial settings and short exposure times. However, the field experiment, conducted in real classrooms, showed that 6500 K CCT was perceived as most appropriate for academic activities and led to the highest percentage of correct answers, while 3500 K CCT was most appropriate for recess activities. Based on these findings, the study proposes three dynamic lighting presets (3500 K for \"easy\"/relaxing, 5000 K for \"standard\"/regular, and 6500 K for \"intensive\"/academic) for smart learning environments. Limitations include the short exposure times in the lab, the need to verify combined effects of illuminance and CCT, the need for longer exposure studies, and the need for studies with different age groups and on total visual appearance.",
